From: Sam Edwards <CFSworks@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: Prevent indefinite RX stall on buffer exhaustion

Backport of the not-yet-merged upstream patch (v4, state "Changes
Requested" in patchwork as of 2026-08, no newer revision posted):
https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/20260401041929.12392-3-CFSworks@gmail.com/
"[PATCH net v4 2/2] net: stmmac: Prevent indefinite RX stall on buffer
exhaustion", companion of commit 0bb05e6adfa9 ("net: stmmac: Prevent
NULL deref when RX memory exhausted") which IS merged and is already in
linux-6.18.y (it is also the fix for CVE-2026-46110).

Note that only 1/2 of that series was taken upstream: the NULL deref is
fixed in mainline, linux-next and net-next, but stmmac_rx() there still
ends with a bare "return count;" and the indefinite stall persists.

Why this is needed on Jetson AGX Xavier (dwmac-dwc-qos-eth, DWMAC 4.10,
single RX queue, single shared IRQ):

Under load (many parallel TCP streams + NVMe writes, i.e. page cache /
page_pool memory pressure) RX dies permanently and silently: the IRQ
count and rx_packets freeze, dmesg stays empty, all MMC error counters
read 0, and only `ip link set end0 down/up` recovers. Registers read in
the hung state show the exact mechanism:

  DMA_CH0_STATUS      (0x1160) = 0x00000484  -> bit 7 RBU set
  DMA_CH0_INTR_ENABLE (0x1134) = 0x0000D041  -> bit 7 RBUE masked
  DMA_CH0_RX_CONTROL  (0x1108) = 0x00080C01  -> SR=1

stmmac_rx_refill() fails an atomic page_pool allocation and returns
early without rearming descriptors or bumping the RX tail pointer. The
DMA then hits the end of the OWN=0 ring, raises RBU and suspends the
receive process. Because RBUE is not in DMA_CHAN_INTR_DEFAULT_MASK, no
interrupt is generated (per the DesignWare databook, AIS asserts only
for individually enabled abnormal interrupts) - and even if it were,
dwmac4_dma_interrupt() only increments rx_buf_unav_irq without
scheduling NAPI. RI can never fire again since no buffer is ever
filled. NAPI completes, unmasks interrupts, and the driver waits
forever for an interrupt that will never arrive.

Unmasking RBUE is not the right fix: it does not schedule NAPI on
dwmac4, and on xgmac2 it was explicitly removed upstream as an
interrupt storm with "no OOM recovery" (commit "net: stmmac: xgmac2:
disable RBUE in default RX interrupt mask"). The correct recovery is in
software: if stmmac_rx_refill() left too many dirty (unarmed)
descriptors behind, report full budget consumption so NAPI keeps polling
and keeps retrying the refill until it succeeds, at which point the tail
pointer write resumes the suspended DMA receive process.

v4 uses STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH as the threshold rather than "any dirty
descriptor at all", matching the logic already used by the zero-copy
refill path, and avoids burning NAPI cycles on a single stale entry.

Fixes: 47dd7a540b8a ("net: add support for STMicroelectronics Ethernet controllers.")
Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Sam Edwards <CFSworks@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Mateusz Krawczuk <mateusz.krawczuk@cybrixsystems.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
@@ -5402,6 +5402,7 @@ static int stmmac_rx(struct stmmac_priv *priv, int limit, u32 queue)
 	unsigned int desc_size;
 	struct sk_buff *skb = NULL;
 	struct stmmac_xdp_buff ctx;
+	int budget = limit;
 	int xdp_status = 0;
 	int bufsz;

@@ -5669,6 +5670,10 @@ static int stmmac_rx(struct stmmac_priv *priv, int limit, u32 queue)
 	priv->xstats.rx_dropped += rx_dropped;
 	priv->xstats.rx_errors += rx_errors;

+	/* If stmmac_rx_refill() failed, keep trying until it doesn't. */
+	if (unlikely(stmmac_rx_dirty(priv, queue) >= STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH))
+		return budget;
+
 	return count;
 }

